 "scenes": [
  {
   "id": "intro",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Trees have one path between any two nodes. Graphs allow any connections at all: road networks, social networks, the internet, the dependencies between tasks. Before any graph algorithm can run, the graph has to be stored. This video compares the two standard ways."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "head"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Graphs and how to store them"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Here are the seven stages."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "stages"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The vocabulary, the lab's network, the adjacency matrix and adjacency lists."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[0..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Then how to choose between them, directed and weighted graphs, and an exam question."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[4..=6]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "words",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 1: a graph G is a set of vertices V and a set of edges E. We write n for the number of vertices and m for the number of edges."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 1 of 7: the words"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "stages"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "wd"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "wd[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Undirected edges work both ways, like a two-way road. Directed edges have a direction, like a one-way street."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "wd[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "In a weighted graph each edge carries a number: a distance, a time, a cost."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "wd[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The degree of a vertex counts its edges. In a directed graph, in-degree counts edges coming in, out-degree edges going out."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "wd[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A path is a sequence of edges; a cycle is a path that returns to its start; and a graph is connected if every vertex can reach every other."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "wd[4..=5]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "G = (V, E): n vertices, m edges"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "lab",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 2: the lab's communication network. Ten devices, A to J, and twelve direct links."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 2 of 7: the lab's network"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "wd"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "g"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "gd"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "gd[0..=1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Count the degrees. A links to B and C: degree 2. E links to B, C and H: degree 3."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.A"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.E",
       "delay": 0.4
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "gd[2]",
       "delay": 0.5
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Add all ten degrees and you get 24, exactly twice the 12 edges, because every edge is counted once at each of its two ends. This is the handshake lemma: the degrees always add up to 2m."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "gd[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "sum of degrees = 2m"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "mat",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 3: the adjacency matrix is an n by n table with a 1 in row u, column v when u and v are joined."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 3 of 7: the adjacency matrix"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "gd"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "mx"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mx[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Row A has 1s in columns B and C. Row J has 1s in G, H and I."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mx[0]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mx[9]",
       "delay": 0.5
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "For an undirected graph it is symmetric: A to B and B to A are the same edge. The 1s add up to 2m, 24."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mx[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Asking whether two vertices are joined is one lookup, Theta of 1. But the table always needs n squared cells, 100 here, however few edges there are, and listing a vertex's neighbours means scanning a whole row."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "matrix: Θ(n²) space, Θ(1) edge test"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "list",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 4: adjacency lists keep, for each vertex, a list of its neighbours."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 4 of 7: adjacency lists"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "mx"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "al"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "al[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A's list holds B and C. E's holds B, C and H."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "al[0]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "al[4]",
       "delay": 0.5
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Each undirected edge appears twice, once in each endpoint's list: 24 entries, plus 10 list heads."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "al"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Space is Theta of n plus m. Listing a vertex's neighbours costs exactly its degree. Checking a single edge means scanning one list."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "lists: Θ(n + m) space, neighbours in Θ(degree)"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "choose",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 5: which to choose? Space: n squared against n plus m."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 5 of 7: which to choose"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "g"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "al"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "ch"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ch[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Edge tests favour the matrix; listing neighbours favours the lists."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ch[1..=2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Breadth-first and depth-first search look at every vertex's neighbours once. With lists that is Theta of n plus m; with a matrix, Theta of n squared."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ch[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Most real graphs are sparse: a road junction meets a handful of roads, not millions. So adjacency lists are the default, and the matrix suits small or dense graphs."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ch[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "sparse: lists · dense: matrix"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "dir",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 6: a directed graph. Edge 1 to 2 is not edge 2 to 1: they would be two different edges."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 6 of 7: directed and weighted"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "ch"
      },
      {
